The reason the Help button stopped working is that your `<button id="help-btn">` is now inside a `<div>` that also contains your label and input field. 
However, your input field (the "Burn amount") is not wrapped in a `<div>` anymore, and there is no longer an element with id `burn-input` in your DOM before your script runs, so `document.getElementById('burn-input')` is returning `null`, causing subsequent errors and likely preventing the Help button’s script from executing properly.

In your latest `lander.html`, the label and the button container are separated. 
But your script still expects an input field with id `burn-input` directly after the label.

---

 How to Fix (Cleanest Way)

 . Make sure you have this structure:

```html
<label for="burn-input">Burn amount:</label>
<input type="number" id="burn-input" min="" step="">
<div style="margin: em auto  auto; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; max-width: px; width: %;">
 <button id="help-btn" ...>Help</button>
 <a href="/" id="home-btn" ...>QUIT</a>
</div>
```
(Make sure the input is present and not removed!)

---

 . Defensive JS: Check Elements Before Adding Listeners

You can prevent JS errors from blocking other scripts like this:

```js
var burnInput = document.getElementById('burn-input');
if (burnInput) {
 burnInput.addEventListener('keydown', function(event) {
 // ... your code ...
 });
}

var helpBtn = document.getElementById('help-btn');
if (helpBtn) {
 helpBtn.addEventListener('click', function() {
 var helpSection = document.getElementById('help-section');
 if (helpSection.style.display === 'none' || helpSection.style.display === '') {
 helpSection.style.display = 'block';
 this.textContent = "Hide Help";
 } else {
 helpSection.style.display = 'none';
 this.textContent = "Help";
 }
 });
}
```

---

 . TL;DR Version
- Ensure your input field is in the DOM with id `burn-input`!
- Add a null check before adding event listeners in JS.
- Then your Help button will work as expected.

