To fade the background image in CSS, you have a few options depending on what exactly you mean by “fade”:

---

  Option : Use a semi-transparent overlay

Wrap the content in a container and layer a semi-transparent element over the background image.

 CSS:
```css
body {
 background: url("images/background.png") no-repeat center center;
 background-size: cover;
 position: relative;
}

body::before {
 content: "";
 position: fixed;
 top: ;
 left: ;
 height: %;
 width: %;
 background-color: rgba(, , , .); / Adjust opacity here /
 z-index: -;
}
```

This will give the background a “faded” or muted appearance with a colored overlay.

---

  Option : Use CSS `opacity` with `background-image` on a pseudo-element

If you want to specifically fade the image but not the content, separate the image using a pseudo-element:

 CSS:
```css
body {
 position: relative;
 background-color: rgb(, , );
}

body::before {
 content: "";
 background: url("images/background.png") no-repeat center center;
 background-size: cover;
 opacity: .; / Faded background /
 position: fixed;
 top: ;
 left: ;
 height: %;
 width: %;
 z-index: -;
}
```

---

 ️ Don’t do this:
```css
opacity: .;
```
...on `body` or a container with content. That fades everything, including text and images.

